  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **increases** at a rate **inversely proportional** to its **height**.* \n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *increases* at a rate *inversely proportional to the height $h.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{k}{h},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the radius of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{h} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{h} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{(\\frac{k}{h})}{\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{k}{\\pi {r}^{2}h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{\\pi {r}^{2}}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{K}{h}.\n$$",
